-- title:
--   Separability of the X₁(M) q-expansion field over K(j)
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field and let $M$ be a nonzero natural number. Write $F =$ `qExpFunctionFieldC K (CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma1 M)` for the intermediate field of the Laurent series field `LaurentSeries K` over $K$ generated by the set `intFormRatiosC K (CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma1 M)`, that is, by all quotients $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,K\,p_f / \mathrm{intSeriesC}\,K\,p_g$ with $k$ an integer, $f,g$ modular forms of weight $k$ for the image of $\Gamma_1(M)$ in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$, and $p_f,p_g$ power series over $\mathbb{Z}$ satisfying the predicate `IsIntegralQExp` relative to $f$ and to $g$ respectively, subject to $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,K\,p_g \neq 0$; here `intSeriesC` sends an integral power series to a Laurent series over $K$. Let $x \in F$ be an element whose underlying Laurent series equals `jqModC K`, namely $q^{-1}$ times the image in $K[[q]]$ of the integral power series $\mathrm{jNum} = \mathrm{eisenstein4}^3 \cdot \mathrm{dedekindEtaUnitInv}$ under coefficientwise reduction along $\mathbb{Z} \to K$. The conclusion is that $F$ is a separable algebra over the intermediate field $K(x)$ obtained by adjoining $x$ to $K$ inside $F$: every element of $F$ is integral over $K(x)$ with separable minimal polynomial.
--
--   This is the separability half of the statement that the $q$-expansion function field of $X_1(M)$ over an algebraically closed field is a finite separable extension of the rational function field generated by the reduction of the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$; in characteristic $p$ it corresponds to Igusa's theorem that the level-$M$ modular function field is Galois, hence separable, over $K(j)$. It is used in the construction of the natural algebra homomorphisms of $q$-expansion function fields for $\Gamma_1$ and for $\Gamma_H$ levels, and in the finiteness and separability statements attached to the Hecke correspondence.
-- source:
